      "imageAlt": "Ethan hesitates with a flashlight while Leo eagerly leads the way down the basement stairs during a storm.",
      "caption": "Leo’s excitement draws cautious Ethan into the dark basement.",
      "text": "The storm outside rattled the old house, rain tapping against the windows like impatient fingers. The wind howled through the cracks in the shutters, making the place feel alive, as though it were breathing. Ethan stood at the top of the basement stairs, flashlight in hand, staring into the yawning darkness below. The wooden door had groaned when he opened it, its hinges shrieking like a warning not to go further.\n\nHe hesitated, his knuckles white around the flashlight. The smell of damp wood and dust drifted up from the basement, heavy and stale, like the breath of something ancient.\n\n“Are you sure about this?” Ethan asked, his voice low, almost swallowed by the silence of the house.\n\nLeo, two years younger and infinitely bolder, grinned back at him from halfway down the steps. His sneakers squeaked against the wood as he leaned into the shadows. “Of course. Grandpa said nobody’s been down here in years. That means secrets. And secrets mean treasure.”\n\nEthan frowned. He wasn’t afraid of the dark, not really — but the basement had always felt different. It wasn’t just shadows down there; it was the weight of years, of things forgotten and left behind. He remembered how Grandpa used to lock the door, how he’d mutter something about “keeping the past where it belongs.”\n\nThe thought made Ethan’s stomach twist.\n\nLeo tilted his head, his grin widening. “Come on, Ethan. Don’t tell me you’re scared.”\n\n“I’m not scared,” Ethan muttered, though his voice betrayed him. He adjusted the strap of his backpack, more for comfort than necessity. “I just think… maybe Grandpa didn’t want us down there for a reason.”\n\n“Exactly,” Leo said, his eyes gleaming with mischief. “People don’t hide boring stuff. If he locked it up, it’s because it’s important. And if it’s important, it’s worth finding.”\n\nThe wind outside slammed against the house, rattling the doorframe. Ethan flinched, then sighed, tightening his grip on the flashlight. He knew his brother well enough to understand there was no turning back now.\n\n“Fine,” he said, his voice firming. “But if we get caught, it’s your fault.”\n\nLeo laughed, bounding down the last few steps into the darkness. “Deal.”\n\nEthan took a deep breath and followed, the beam of his flashlight slicing through the gloom. The basement swallowed them whole, the storm fading into a distant murmur above."
